-- Passage rg7-evidence-inference-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5a0745b1-5ec2-5359-a0f2-84878d4b04ea', 'rg7-evidence-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Lamplighters$q$, $q$Before electric lights became common, many city streets were lit by gas lamps. Each lamp stood on a tall post and had to be lit by hand every evening. The workers who did this job were called lamplighters.

A lamplighter usually carried a long pole with a small flame or a hook at the end. Walking a set route, he would open a valve on each lamp and reach up to light the gas. Near dawn, he walked the same route again to turn the lamps off. In a large city, hundreds of these workers might be out at the same time, each responsible for dozens of lamps.

The job involved more than lighting flames. Lamplighters cleaned the glass panes, which grew black with soot, and they reported broken posts or leaking pipes. Because they walked the streets at dusk and dawn, they often noticed problems before anyone else did. Residents came to know the lamplighter on their street, and children sometimes followed him on his rounds.

Gas lighting changed city life. Shops could stay open later, and people felt safer walking after dark. Yet the system had limits. Gas lamps gave off a dim, flickering glow, and leaks could be dangerous.

When electric streetlights spread in the early 1900s, cities began switching them on from a central station. Within a few decades, the lamplighter's evening walk had nearly disappeared. A few towns still keep gas lamps in their historic districts. Some even hire a lamplighter for special occasions, as a reminder of how streets once glowed.$q$, 'draft')
