-- Passage rg7-evidence-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('45f04a83-56d8-5722-a194-2c4001a1fa2b', 'rg7-evidence-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Borrowing More Than Books$q$, $q$At the Millbrook Public Library, the most popular item last spring was not a book. It was a pressure washer. The library had added it to a new collection called the Library of Things, which lends out tools, games and equipment the same way it lends novels.

The idea began with a survey. Librarian Adaeze Okafor asked residents what they had bought, used once and then stored away. Many people listed the same items: ladders, sewing machines, camping tents and cake pans in unusual shapes.

Okafor and a team of volunteers collected donations and bought a few items with a small grant. Each object received a barcode and a sturdy bin. Borrowers can keep most items for one week. Larger tools, like the pressure washer, must be returned in three days so more people get a turn.

The collection has grown to more than 150 objects. A waiting list for the telescope now stretches into the summer. Teenagers borrow the electric keyboard and the video camera for school projects. A local builder teaches a monthly class on using the power drill safely, and only people who complete the class may borrow it.

Not everything has worked. A popcorn machine came back broken twice, and the library removed it. Staff also learned that some kits, such as a board game with dozens of pieces, need a checklist. The list lets borrowers confirm that every piece is returned.

Okafor says the program saves money and space for families. It has also brought new visitors through the doors. Several people who came in to borrow a tool left with their first library card.$q$, 'draft')
