-- Passage rg7-evidence-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8d455f9d-ae93-5a51-8119-0094b90628c0', 'rg7-evidence-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Bringing a Faded Mural Back$q$, $q$For forty years, a painted mural has covered the side wall of the community center on Alder Street. It shows a river winding past houses, gardens and a busy market. By last year, much of it had faded to pale blues and grays. Several sections had cracked, and in one corner the paint had peeled away entirely.

A team of art conservators was hired to repair it. Their first step was not painting. Instead, they spent two weeks studying the wall. They photographed every section, tested small spots to identify the original paints and searched the center's files for the artist's sketches. They wanted to learn what the mural had looked like when it was new.

The sketches turned out to be important. In the peeling corner, the photographs showed only blank plaster. The sketches revealed that the corner once held a group of children flying kites. Without them, the team would have had to guess.

Next, the conservators cleaned the wall with soft brushes and mild soap. Strong chemicals could have removed dirt faster, but they might also have removed original paint. The team then filled the cracks. They matched each color by mixing small test batches until the new paint looked right beside the old.

Lead conservator Tomas Reyes-Lindqvist explained that the goal was to repair the mural, not to improve it. The team did not add new figures or change the colors to suit modern tastes. Once the work was finished, they coated the surface with a clear layer that blocks sunlight.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
